---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import WhatsAppButton from "../../components/WhatsAppButton.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";
import Button from "../../components/Button.astro";
import ArticleNext from "../../components/ArticleNext.astro";
import { customerFirstArticle as article, onlinePresenceArticle, ctaLink } from "../../config";
import "../../styles/article-prose.css";

const { toc, journey } = article;
---

<Layout
  title={article.seoTitle}
  description={article.description}
  article={{ headline: article.title, datePublished: article.datePublished }}
>
  <Header />

  <!--
    Deliberately an <article>, not a <section>: Layout's depth-scroll effect
    targets `main > section` and dimming a long read while you're in it is
    hostile. The section ids inside still feed the shared scroll-spy, which is
    what lights up the table of contents in the left rail.
  -->
  <main id="main">
    <article class="pb-10 pt-32 sm:pt-36">
      <div class="container">
        <a
          href="/#articles"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-muted transition-colors hover:text-text"
        >
          <Icon name="arrow" class="h-4 w-4 rotate-180" />
          Back to home
        </a>

        <header class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-3xl text-center">
          <p data-reveal class="eyebrow">Article</p>

          <h1
            data-reveal
            style="--reveal-delay:60ms"
            class="mt-3 text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-text sm:text-[2.7rem] sm:leading-[1.1]"
          >
            {article.title}
          </h1>

          <p
            data-reveal
            style="--reveal-delay:120ms"
            class="mx-auto mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted"
          >
            {article.lead}
          </p>
        </header>

        <hr class="mt-12 border-0 border-t border-border" />

        <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,19rem)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-14">
          <!-- Left rail: the map of the article, plus the progression the whole
               argument is organised around. Both stay in view while you read. -->
          <aside
            data-reveal
            class="space-y-5 lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:max-h-[calc(100vh-7rem)] lg:self-start lg:overflow-y-auto lg:pr-1"
          >
            <nav
              aria-labelledby="toc-heading"
              class="bg-surface/50 rounded-[var(--radius-lg)] border border-border p-6"
            >
              <h2 id="toc-heading" class="text-base font-bold text-text">In this article</h2>
              <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm">
                {
                  toc.map((item, i) => (
                    <li>
                      <a
                        href={`#${item.id}`}
                        data-spy-link
                        class="flex gap-2.5 leading-snug text-muted transition-colors hover:text-text [&.is-active]:font-semibold [&.is-active]:text-primary"
                      >
                        <span class="shrink-0 tabular-nums text-faint">{i + 1}.</span>
                        <span>{item.label}</span>
                      </a>
                    </li>
                  ))
                }
              </ol>
            </nav>

            <div class="bg-surface/50 rounded-[var(--radius-lg)] border border-border p-6">
              <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-text">{journey.title}</h2>
              <ol class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm">
                {
                  journey.stages.map((stage, i) => (
                    <li class="flex items-center gap-2.5 font-semibold text-text">
                      <span class="w-4 shrink-0 font-normal tabular-nums text-faint">{i + 1}.</span>
                      {stage}
                    </li>
                  ))
                }
              </ol>
              <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{journey.note}</p>
            </div>
          </aside>

          <div class="article-prose min-w-0 max-w-[68ch]">
            <section id="introduction">
              <div class="callout callout--tagged">
                <p class="eyebrow">What you'll take away</p>
                <p>
                  By the end, you'll have a clear understanding of your customer's journey and what
                  they need at any given stage.
                </p>
                <p>
                  You'll also understand how to build a website that supports this journey,
                  accurately represents your business, and helps turn visitors into customers.
                </p>
              </div>

              <p>
                I went to my developer and said, “I run a Google Ads management agency and I need a
                website.”
              </p>
              <p>He made one.</p>
              <p>
                The result looked impressive. It had all the things you'd expect from a modern
                agency website: cards, visuals, taglines, sections and polished design.
              </p>
              <p>
                But when I started looking beneath the surface, I found a problem. The website
                contained processes that weren't actually ours. It made claims without explaining
                the reasoning behind them. Its trust signals were no different from those you'd find
                on countless other agency websites.
              </p>
              <p>
                Much of the content was essentially filling the expected spaces on a website rather
                than representing what was actually true about our business.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>It looked like a website. It didn't yet represent the business.</strong>
              </p>
              <p>Later, when talking to my developer, I realised this wasn't unusual.</p>
              <p>Business owners often come to developers with a similar request:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p>“I run a business. Can you build me a website?”</p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                That's the entire brief. The developer is then expected to turn that vague request
                into a finished website.
              </p>
              <p>The owner may have an intuitive understanding of:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p>“This is who we are. This is what we do. This is why customers choose us.”</p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                But unless that understanding is communicated, the developer doesn't have access to
                it.
              </p>
              <p>
                And the developer can't manufacture that knowledge simply by being good at web
                development.
              </p>
              <p>
                Therefore the developer relies on assumptions to fill the missing information, often
                based on:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>templates</li>
                <li>industry conventions</li>
                <li>previous websites</li>
                <li>generic copy</li>
                <li>AI-generated copy</li>
                <li>generic design patterns</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                And that's how you get the website that looks appropriate for the category but
                doesn't really represent the business.
              </p>

              <blockquote class="pull">
                <p>
                  When the purpose and requirements of a website remain ambiguous, the ambiguity
                  doesn't disappear. Someone has to resolve it, usually through assumptions.
                </p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                A developer may know that they need more information, but knowing what to ask, how
                to ask it, and in what order is itself ambiguous.
              </p>
              <p><strong>“Tell me about your business” isn't enough.</strong></p>
              <p>
                <strong
                  >The useful questions are determined by the decisions the website eventually has
                  to support.</strong
                >
              </p>
              <p>
                A website can be built by a developer, an agency, or increasingly by AI. You don't
                need to know how to code to get one built well.
              </p>
              <p>But you do need to understand <strong>what you're asking to be built</strong>.</p>
              <p>This article is about developing that understanding.</p>
              <p>
                That's what changed when I built version 2 of the Ad-Ascent website. I didn't start
                with the website. I started with a much simpler question:
              </p>
              <p><strong>Who is this website for?</strong></p>
              <p>
                Then I worked backwards, applying the same reasoning to every component of the
                website.
              </p>
              <p><strong>That way of thinking is what we'll explore in this article.</strong></p>
            </section>

            <section id="asset">
              <h2>A website is an asset, not just a collection of pages</h2>
              <p>
                A website is an asset that gives a business its own environment on the internet; one
                where people can explore the business more deeply.
              </p>
              <p>
                They can learn about what it offers, understand how it works, look at its work,
                evaluate it against other options, decide whether there is a fit, and take the next
                step without needing to speak to the business at all.
              </p>
              <p>
                Notice that we're describing the website from the visitor's perspective. That's
                intentional.
              </p>
              <p>
                Often developers think about a website in terms of what the business wants to put on
                it: an About page, a Services page, some photographs, a few testimonials, and a
                contact form. But those things are only meaningful because of what they enable the
                people visiting the website to do.
              </p>
              <p>And those people won't all arrive for the same reason.</p>
              <p>
                <strong>Some may already be considering your business.</strong> They want to understand
                what you offer, decide whether there's a fit, compare you with their other options, and
                determine whether they can trust you enough to move forward.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Others may know little or nothing about your business.</strong> They may have
                encountered you while exploring a problem, considering possible solutions, looking for
                information, or simply exploring the internet.
              </p>
              <p>
                In either case, they arrive at a different point in their relationship with the
                business.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong
                  >The website needs to meet them where they are and give them what they need to
                  move forward.</strong
                >
              </p>
            </section>

            <section id="journey">
              <h2>The Customer’s Journey</h2>
              <p>
                Imagine you're driving your car and it suddenly starts making an unfamiliar noise.
              </p>
              <p>Your first thought probably isn't:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p><em>“Which mechanic should I choose?”</em></p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>It's more likely:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p><strong>“What's wrong with my car?”</strong></p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>You want to understand the problem.</p>
              <p>
                You might listen to the noise more carefully, search online, ask someone you know,
                or take the car to a mechanic. And these don't always happen separately.
              </p>
              <p>
                You might take the car to a mechanic precisely because you don't understand what's
                wrong. The mechanic examines it and tells you what the problem is, what caused it,
                and what needs to be done.
              </p>
              <p>
                In that situation, the mechanic isn't only providing the service. They're also
                helping you <strong>understand the problem you're paying them to solve</strong>.
              </p>
              <p>
                You may not know enough to fix it yourself. But if the mechanic can explain the
                problem clearly, show you what they've found, and explain why a particular repair is
                appropriate, you have a better basis for evaluating their recommendation.
              </p>
              <p>Now you're dealing with another question:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p><strong>“Is this the person I want to trust with my car?”</strong></p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                You might ask questions. You might look at their experience or previous work. You
                might look at reviews or ask someone for a recommendation. You might pay attention
                to how they diagnose the problem and explain what they're proposing to do.
              </p>
              <p>As you learn more, you begin to form an impression of them.</p>
              <p>Eventually, you may reach a point where you think:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p>
                  <strong
                    >“I think these people know what they're doing. I'm comfortable leaving my car
                    with them.”</strong
                  >
                </p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>You've developed enough trust to move forward.</p>
              <p>Notice what just happened.</p>
              <p>
                You didn't go directly from <strong>having a problem</strong> to <strong
                  >choosing a provider</strong
                >.
              </p>
              <p>
                You needed to understand your situation, determine whether the proposed service was
                appropriate, evaluate the person providing it, and develop enough confidence to
                trust them with something that mattered to you.
              </p>
              <p>
                Only then were you ready to choose. And if the experience was good, you might later
                recommend the mechanic to someone else.
              </p>
              <p>
                The exact process may vary from one situation to another, but there is a useful
                progression underneath:
              </p>

              <div class="callout">
                <ol class="journey" aria-label="The customer's journey">
                  {journey.stages.map((stage) => <li>{stage}</li>)}
                </ol>
              </div>

              <p>
                These stages help us think about the different things people may need as their
                relationship with a business develops.
              </p>
              <p>
                A website can support that progression. It can help people discover a business,
                understand what it offers, evaluate whether it's right for them, develop trust, and
                take the next step. Although it's important to note that people may arrive at
                different stages, and some stages may take place elsewhere.
              </p>
              <p>
                Another important nuance is that these stages often overlap in real life. We're
                separating the concepts to understand what a website needs to help people do.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong
                  >The important question is what people need at any given stage, and how the
                  website can help them move forward.</strong
                >
              </p>
              <p>That's what we're going to explore next.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="understand">
              <h2>Helping visitors understand your business</h2>
              <p>
                Imagine you've arrived at the website of a business you've never encountered before.
              </p>
              <p>
                Before you can decide whether you want to work with them, you need to form some
                basic understanding of what you're looking at.
              </p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p><strong>Who are they?</strong></p>
                <p><strong>What do they do?</strong></p>
                <p><strong>Who do they do it for?</strong></p>
                <p><strong>What exactly do they offer?</strong></p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>And perhaps:</p>
              <p><strong>Is this even relevant to me?</strong></p>
              <p>
                If the website doesn't make these things reasonably clear, it becomes difficult to
                move any further.
              </p>
              <p>
                And so one of the first jobs a website needs to do is <strong
                  >help someone form a clear picture of the business</strong
                >.
              </p>
              <p>
                This is where the homepage becomes important. The homepage is often the first
                substantial place where someone encounters the business, but its job isn't
                necessarily to explain everything the business does.
              </p>
              <p>
                Its job is to help the visitor understand <strong>what they're looking at</strong> and
                give them enough context to decide where to go next.
              </p>
              <p>For example, imagine a Google Ads agency whose homepage simply says:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p><strong>We help businesses grow.</strong></p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                The statement may sound appealing, but it doesn't give the visitor much to work
                with. They may still be wondering:
              </p>
              <p>
                <em>“What do you actually do?”</em><br />
                <em>“What kind of businesses do you work with?”</em><br />
                <em>“What does ‘grow’ mean here?”</em><br />
                <em>“Is this something I need?”</em>
              </p>
              <p>Now imagine the homepage provides a more specific explanation:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p>
                  <strong
                    >We help small and medium-sized businesses attract and convert customers through
                    Google ads and websites.</strong
                  >
                </p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                The visitor now has a clearer picture of what the business does, whom it serves, and
                how its services might help.
              </p>
              <p>
                They can begin to decide whether the business is relevant to them and whether they
                want to learn more.
              </p>
              <p>But the homepage doesn't necessarily need to explain everything in detail.</p>
              <p>
                For example, a visitor might need a brief introduction to a service before deciding
                whether to explore it further. That introduction could appear on the homepage, with
                a link to a dedicated page explaining the service in greater depth.
              </p>
              <p>
                Other information might belong in a different section of the homepage or on another
                page entirely.
              </p>

              <blockquote class="pull">
                <p>
                  The need for the information comes before the decision about where that
                  information should live.
                </p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                Some information belongs on the homepage because it helps establish the basic
                picture of the business.
              </p>
              <p>Some requires a deeper explanation.</p>
              <p>Some may deserve its own page.</p>
              <p>
                There is no universal answer. The structure should emerge from what the visitor
                needs to understand, rather than from a standard list of pages that websites are
                supposed to have.
              </p>
              <p>
                We're deciding what someone needs to understand about the business in order to move
                forward in their relationship with it.
              </p>
              <p>
                Once visitors start to form an understanding of the business and its offering,
                another question becomes important:
              </p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p><strong>“Is this actually right for me?”</strong></p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>And that takes us to the next stage: <strong>evaluation</strong>.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="evaluate">
              <h2>What do visitors need to evaluate your business?</h2>
              <p>Let's say you're considering hiring a service provider.</p>
              <p>
                <strong
                  >What would help you determine whether the service provider is right for you?</strong
                >
              </p>
              <p>
                You might want to know whether they've handled situations like yours or done work
                similar to what you're looking for. You might want to know who will actually be
                doing the work and what experience or qualifications they bring. You might also want
                to understand how the provider approaches a problem and what their working process
                involves. Or perhaps something completely different.
              </p>
              <p>What matters will vary from one situation to another.</p>
              <p>
                The important thing to note is that, at the evaluation stage, the question becomes:
              </p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p>
                  <strong
                    >“What would help the people we're trying to serve determine whether this
                    business is right for them?”</strong
                  >
                </p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>The answer determines what the website needs to provide.</p>
              <p>
                If visitors need to see previous work, the website might need a portfolio or case
                studies. If the business's approach is an important consideration, it might need to
                explain that approach. If the process affects the customer's decision, the website
                might need to make it clear.
              </p>
              <p>
                These don't necessarily need to become separate pages. A brief explanation might be
                sufficient in one context, while another business might need a dedicated page with
                considerably more detail.
              </p>

              <blockquote class="pull">
                <p>The requirement comes first. The website element comes afterwards.</p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                We're not simply collecting information the business has available or adding
                components because other websites have them.
              </p>
              <p>
                We're determining <strong
                  >what visitors need to know in order to evaluate the business</strong
                >, and then deciding how the website can best provide that information.
              </p>
              <p>
                But even when visitors have enough information to judge that a business might be
                right for them, one question can remain:
              </p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p>
                  <strong>“I think this could be right for me but should I work with them?”</strong>
                </p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>That's where we turn to <strong>trust</strong>.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="confidence">
              <h2>Building confidence in the working relationship</h2>
              <p>
                By this point, someone may understand your business, recognise that your offering is
                relevant to their situation, and believe that you have the capability to help them.
              </p>
              <p>They're beginning to imagine working with you.</p>
              <p>But some uncertainty may remain.</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p>
                  <strong
                    >“This looks like what I need. But what would working with these people actually
                    involve?”</strong
                  >
                </p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>They may be wondering:</p>
              <ul>
                <li>How much will it cost?</li>
                <li>How much time and effort will I need to invest?</li>
                <li>How long will the service provider need to deliver?</li>
                <li>What will happen once we start working together?</li>
                <li>What can I expect from them?</li>
                <li>How will communication work?</li>
                <li>What happens if something changes?</li>
              </ul>
              <p>These questions are different from those involved in evaluating the business.</p>
              <p>
                During evaluation, the visitor is trying to determine whether the business and its
                offering are right for them.
              </p>
              <p>
                Now, they're considering <strong
                  >what choosing that business would mean for them</strong
                >.
              </p>
              <p>
                They may believe the business can deliver what they need, yet remain uncertain about
                the commitment involved, the experience of working together, or whether the
                arrangement will fit their circumstances.
              </p>
              <p>And this is where the website can do something different.</p>
              <p>
                It can help the visitor <strong
                  >build confidence in the business and in the relationship they are considering</strong
                >.
              </p>
              <p>
                Some of that confidence may come from things we've already encountered while
                thinking about evaluation: previous work, experience, qualifications, or evidence of
                capability.
              </p>
              <p>
                But confidence can also come from helping someone understand what happens after they
                choose you.
              </p>
              <p>
                For example, if the process is explained clearly, the visitor can begin to imagine
                what working with the business will actually be like.
              </p>
              <p>They might understand:</p>
              <ul>
                <li>what happens first</li>
                <li>what happens next</li>
                <li>how long different stages might take</li>
                <li>what the business will need from them</li>
                <li>what they can expect from the business</li>
                <li>how communication will work</li>
                <li>what happens if something changes</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                The exact things that need to be explained will depend on the business and the
                relationship it is asking someone to enter.
              </p>
              <p>The point isn't to add a generic <strong>“Trust”</strong> section to a website.</p>
              <p>It's to ask:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p>
                  <strong
                    >“What uncertainty might still prevent someone from feeling comfortable moving
                    forward, and what could the website do to reduce it?”</strong
                  >
                </p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                Sometimes the answer will be evidence.<br />
                Sometimes it will be transparency.<br />
                Sometimes it will be the people behind the business.<br />
                Sometimes it will be a clear explanation of the process and expectations.<br />
                Sometimes it will be something else entirely.
              </p>
              <p>
                Trust, then, isn't something you create by adding a few testimonials or putting a
                “trusted” badge on the page.
              </p>
              <p>
                It develops from the overall experience the business gives someone: what it says,
                what it can demonstrate, how clearly it communicates, and how well it sets
                expectations about the relationship.
              </p>
              <p>
                And once someone has understood the business, evaluated the offering, and developed
                enough confidence in both the business and what working with it would involve, we
                reach a much simpler question:
              </p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p><strong>“How do I take the next step?”</strong></p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>That is what the website needs to facilitate next.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="next-step">
              <h2>The next step</h2>
              <p>Once a visitor is ready to move forward, what should happen next?</p>
              <p>For a service business, that might mean inviting someone to:</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Call the business.</li>
                <li>Send an enquiry.</li>
                <li>Request a quote.</li>
                <li>Book a consultation.</li>
                <li>Schedule an appointment.</li>
                <li>Start a project.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                The appropriate next step depends on the business, what it offers, and what the
                visitor needs to do to move forward.
              </p>
              <p>
                A business might want someone to book a consultation. Another might need an enquiry
                before it can provide a quote. A business that sells directly online might want the
                visitor to purchase immediately.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong
                  >The next step needs to make sense for both the visitor and the business.</strong
                >
              </p>
              <p>This is where the <strong>call to action</strong>, or CTA, comes in.</p>
              <p>A CTA is essentially the website saying:</p>

              <blockquote class="note">
                <p><strong>“If you're ready to take this next step, here's how.”</strong></p>
              </blockquote>

              <p>
                A CTA invites the action, while the website needs some form of functionality to
                enable it.
              </p>
              <p>
                A <em>Book a consultation</em> button might lead to a booking system.<br />
                A <em>Request a quote</em> button might open an enquiry form.<br />
                A <em>Call us</em> button might initiate a phone call.
              </p>
              <p>
                The appropriate solution depends on the action the visitor needs to take and how the
                business needs to handle it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section id="principles">
              <div class="callout">
                <h2>The principles of a Customer-First Website</h2>
                <p>
                  The principles we've been applying throughout this article can now be stated
                  explicitly:
                </p>
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <strong>Start with the customer, not the website.</strong> Understand who the website
                    is for, what they need, and where they are in their relationship with the business.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>Understand the customer's journey.</strong> Consider what people need as they
                    discover, understand, evaluate, and decide whether to work with a business. Remember
                    that this journey isn't necessarily linear.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>Let customer needs determine website requirements.</strong> Identify what
                    visitors need to understand, what would help them evaluate the business, what could
                    give them confidence, and what they need to do next.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>Derive website components from those requirements.</strong> Decide what information
                    to provide, how to organise it, how to present it, and what functionality is needed.
                    Don't add pages or features simply because other websites have them.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>Represent the business accurately.</strong> The website should reflect what
                    the business actually offers, how it works, and what customers can reasonably expect
                    from it.
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <p>
                These principles give us a way to reason about what makes a business website
                effective. They move us beyond asking what a website should look like or which pages
                it should contain, towards understanding what it needs to accomplish.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong
                  >The next step is to translate these principles into concrete website
                  requirements:</strong
                >
              </p>
              <ol>
                <li>Content</li>
                <li>Organisation</li>
                <li>Design and presentation</li>
                <li>Functionality</li>
              </ol>
              <p>That's what we'll explore in the next article.</p>
            </section>

